---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Button from "../ui/Button.astro";
import heroOfficer from "../../assets/hero/hero-officer.png";

/**
 * The homepage hero — full rebuild, replacing the old side-by-side
 * "framed photo + Picsum placeholder" layout entirely. Full-bleed real
 * photograph (a House of Guards officer in a client building lobby,
 * genuine content, not a placeholder), with the headline/subhead/CTAs
 * sitting in the photo's own right-hand negative space on desktop.
 *
 * REAL PHOTO GEOMETRY, checked not guessed — the source file is
 * 1672×941 (a 1.777:1 / 16:9-ish crop, confirmed via `sharp` metadata,
 * NOT the ~1.335:1 I'd have guessed from a scaled-down thumbnail).
 * The officer occupies roughly the left 0–55% of the frame; his face
 * sits at approximately x:20–33%, y:5–30%; the "House of Guards" print
 * on his jacket sits around y:58–65%. Everything right of ~55% is the
 * blurred, bright lobby — the intended text zone on desktop.
 *
 * `object-[16%_22%]` is ONE combined value used at every breakpoint,
 * not a per-breakpoint override — derived from `object-fit: cover`'s
 * own math (scale = max(container_w/img_w, container_h/img_h)) run
 * against real numbers, not assumed to "just work":
 *   - Narrow mobile (390×640): height-driven scale (0.680) → ZERO
 *     vertical crop, ~34% of the original width visible. Centered
 *     (50%) would land on [33%,67%] — mostly empty lobby, his face
 *     entirely cropped out. X=16% moves the window to roughly
 *     [8%,42%] — face and shoulders comfortably inside, margin on both
 *     sides even at 320px width (checked separately: [11.5%,40%]).
 *   - `lg` floor, 1024×676 (88svh of 768, clamped to the 640 floor):
 *     width-driven scale (0.718) needs a small width crop; X=16% keeps
 *     [2.4%,88%] visible — virtually the whole frame, officer and
 *     lobby both intact.
 *   - Wide desktop, 1280×704 and 1920×880 (the `max-h` ceiling): both
 *     land very close to the image's own native ratio, so cropping is
 *     minimal on EITHER axis regardless of object-position — Y matters
 *     more here (a few % top/bottom), and Y=22% keeps his head fully
 *     in frame while trimming a little off the bottom (waist/hands,
 *     never the branding text at ~60%).
 * Because mobile crops are always height-driven (X matters, Y is a
 * no-op) and desktop crops are always width-driven-or-negligible
 * (Y matters, X is mostly a no-op), one value serves both regimes —
 * confirmed by working through the arithmetic for each case above
 * rather than assuming it generalises.
 *
 * SECTION HEIGHT — `h-[640px] sm:h-[720px]` below `lg`, viewport-
 * relative from `lg` up: `lg:h-[88svh] lg:min-h-[640px] lg:max-h-[880px]`.
 * Checked against real laptop heights so the hero doesn't eat the
 * whole screen: 1366×768 → 676px hero, 92px of the next section
 * already peeking below the fold. 1440×900 → 792px/108px peek.
 * 1024×768 (the `lg` breakpoint itself, matching Header's nav
 * breakpoint) → 676px, within the clamp. 1920×1080 hits the 880px
 * ceiling, leaving 200px of peek rather than growing further. Fixed
 * px (not svh) below `lg` deliberately — avoids interacting with
 * mobile browser chrome/dynamic-toolbar resize quirks on what's
 * already a bottom-anchored overlay composition.
 *
 * FULL-IMAGE TINT — a second, separate `bg-ink/28` layer sitting
 * between `<Image>` and the text panel, covering the ENTIRE section
 * (not just behind the text) for a moodier, more premium feel —
 * independent of the text-panel scrim below it, which now carries the
 * rebrand's own Electric Blue rather than Guard Green Deep (see TEXT-
 * PANEL SCRIM below) but is otherwise unchanged by this pass — this
 * tint layer itself uses Ink, not one of the four new colours, and
 * wasn't touched. Ink (`#0d0d0d`, rgb(13,13,13))
 * was used rather than a new raw `#000000` specifically because it's
 * ALREADY a genuinely neutral near-black — R=G=B, zero hue, checked
 * before reusing it rather than assumed — so this stays inside the
 * existing token system with no exception needed (this was originally
 * Guard Green Deep at 18% opacity; switched to Ink at 28% per direct
 * feedback that the tint should read as neutral black, not green, and
 * be more pronounced). Decorative only (`aria-hidden`, `pointer-
 * events-none`); no explicit z-index needed, since an `absolute`
 * element later in the DOM than `<Image>` but earlier than the `z-10`
 * content wrapper already paints above the image and below the text,
 * which is all this layer needs to do.
 *
 * TEXT-PANEL SCRIM — a FLAT, uniform-opacity Electric-Blue-at-85%
 * panel behind the text (rebranded off Guard Green Deep), not a
 * percentage-based gradient. A gradient's safe-contrast zone depends
 * on exactly where it sits relative to the text, which in turn depends
 * on the panel's real rendered height — unknowable without a live
 * render. A flat panel's contrast is the same at every pixel behind
 * the text regardless of exact dimensions, which matters given nothing
 * here can be visually verified before shipping.
 *
 * Real WCAG math, worst case (the underlying photo assumed PURE WHITE
 * #FFFFFF, the most extreme case it could ever show through), and
 * RE-DERIVED FROM SCRATCH for this pass — do NOT assume the previous
 * ~12.72:1 figure carries over just because the compositing MECHANISM
 * (photo → Ink tint → an 85%-opacity scrim on top) is unchanged; the
 * scrim's own colour changed, which is exactly the kind of thing this
 * project's own standing rule says to re-derive rather than carry
 * forward: rgb(13,13,13)@28% over white → rgb(187,187,187), then
 * Electric Blue rgb(37,99,235)@85% over THAT → rgb(59.5,112.2,227.8)
 * → white-text contrast ≈ **4.53:1**.
 *
 * FLAG, NOT SILENTLY SHIPPED — this is a genuine PASS (clears the
 * 4.5:1 AA floor) but by a razor-thin margin, a real, disclosed
 * REGRESSION from the previous figure (12.72:1 → 4.53:1), not a wash.
 * The number itself is mathematically sound — it's already the
 * worst-case derivation (a lighter underlying photo colour than pure
 * white would only make the panel darker and the margin bigger, never
 * smaller) — but 4.53:1 leaves almost no room for anything else that
 * could nudge real-world contrast down even slightly (a slightly
 * different browser colour-management pass, a headline/subhead font
 * that renders a hair lighter than assumed). This did NOT get the
 * "too thin to trust, swap something" treatment this codebase gives
 * comparably thin margins elsewhere (e.g. Guard Green Secondary's own
 * ~3.06:1 focus-ring case) because doing so would mean changing the
 * panel's own opacity beyond what this pass was asked to touch — flag
 * it here instead, plainly, for a real decision: if a wider safety
 * margin is wanted, bumping the scrim from 85% to 90% opacity lifts
 * this to ~4.74:1 with real headroom, checked and ready to apply, not
 * shipped speculatively. `outline-light`'s white border/text and the
 * default-safe-focus-ring question (`on-dark` swaps the ring to Paper
 * — kept here for the same reason StatStrip/the header info bar keep
 * it against Electric Blue: Ink-on-this-panel is ~4.29:1, which clears
 * the 3:1 UI floor on its own with real margin this time, unlike
 * Guard Green Secondary's old ~3.06:1, but Paper's own ~4.53:1 is
 * still the stronger of the two, so `.on-dark` stays rather than being
 * removed) both sit on this same combined panel, so the same 4.53:1
 * covers them too. Button.astro's `primary` variant is unaffected
 * either way — its own opaque Amber fill sits on top of both layers
 * regardless, and its own Ink-on-Amber ~9.05:1 has nothing to do with
 * the panel underneath it.
 *
 * SEAM RISK — the same disclosed, unresolved risk this section
 * flagged before the rebrand, re-checked rather than assumed to have
 * resolved itself just because the scrim's hue changed: the tint-only
 * worst case (outside the panel) is a NEUTRAL grey, rgb(187,187,187)
 * — zero hue — while the panel itself is a saturated Electric Blue.
 * That's still a genuine HUE shift at the boundary on top of the usual
 * brightness step (a blue rectangle now, rather than a green one, but
 * the same category of risk), which still risks reading as a
 * differently-coloured rectangle pasted onto the image rather than a
 * natural darkening. Not fixed here — the brief kept the same
 * constraint as before, that the scrim carries its own colour and the
 * two layers stay separate — but check this specific edge live before
 * considering it finished; the two most likely fixes, if it reads as a
 * visible seam, are matching the scrim to Ink instead of Electric
 * Blue, or a soft edge blend, neither implemented speculatively here.
 *
 * HEADER LEGIBILITY — the tint only affects the hero `<section>`
 * itself. `Header.astro`'s info bar and main nav are separate, fully
 * opaque, normal-flow elements rendered BEFORE `<main>` in
 * `BaseLayout.astro` (`<Header /><main><slot /></main>`) — never
 * stacked over the hero image at any scroll position, confirmed via
 * source rather than assumed, since nothing about this header is a
 * transparent overlay the way a true floating hero header would be.
 *
 * HEADLINE SIZE — `text-h3 sm:text-h2`, stepped down one named tier at
 * each breakpoint from the first version of this component (base
 * text-h2, sm: text-h1) per direct feedback that the original read
 * too large against the subhead/CTAs. Still deliberately not this
 * system's own 88px "hero statement only" display size — already
 * ruled out in the original build given this headline's
 * length, which would wrap into enough lines at 88px to risk the
 * exact below-the-fold problem the section-height math above works
 * around. The text panel has no explicit height anywhere — `w-full`/
 * `lg:max-w-lg` plus padding only — it's sized purely by its own
 * content, so this reduction shrinks the panel automatically; no
 * separate height adjustment was needed or made.
 *
 * SUBHEAD SIZE — `text-caption` (14px), stepped down a SECOND named
 * tier per a direct follow-up request to shrink it further: first
 * pass went `text-body-lg` (20px) → `text-body` (17px), this pass
 * `text-body` → `text-caption`. Read the current class each time
 * before changing it rather than guessing a size. Headline and button
 * sizes are untouched. Worth flagging plainly: `text-caption` is this
 * system's nav-link/meta-line size (see `NavLink.astro`, the header
 * info bar) — genuinely small for a hero subhead paragraph, not just
 * "a bit smaller." Shipped as explicitly requested, not silently
 * pushed back on. The text panel has no explicit height, so it
 * shrinks to match automatically — confirmed in the compiled output,
 * not assumed, same as every other size change on this component.
 *
 * Mobile does NOT reuse the desktop's right-column placement — a
 * full-width band anchored to the bottom of the image (edge-to-edge,
 * no side margins) is its own composition, not a shrunk desktop
 * layout, per the brief. `outline-light` works identically in both
 * compositions since both sit on the same flat dark panel — no
 * variant-switching between breakpoints needed.
 *
 * No eyebrow label — `SectionHeading` deliberately has no eyebrow slot
 * sitewide (see CLAUDE.md's hard rules), and there's no content here
 * that earns a label the rest of the site treats with suspicion.
 *
 * `data-hero-reveal` markers are read by initHeroMuster()
 * (`src/lib/heroMuster.ts`, called once from index.astro) for the
 * load-triggered (NOT scroll-triggered — this content is already in
 * view on load) staggered fade/rise of headline → subhead → CTA row.
 */
---

<section class="relative isolate h-[640px] overflow-hidden sm:h-[720px] lg:h-[88svh] lg:min-h-[640px] lg:max-h-[880px]">
  <Image
    src={heroOfficer}
    alt="A House of Guards officer standing in a client building lobby"
    class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover object-[16%_22%]"
    widths={[480, 768, 1024, 1440, 1672]}
    sizes="100vw"
    format="webp"
    quality={82}
    loading="eager"
    fetchpriority="high"
  />

  <div aria-hidden="true" class="bg-ink/28 pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"></div>

  <div class="relative z-10 flex h-full flex-col justify-end lg:mx-auto lg:max-w-7xl lg:flex-row lg:items-center lg:justify-end lg:px-12">
    <div
      class="on-dark bg-electric-blue/85 w-full px-6 py-8 sm:px-10 sm:py-10 lg:w-auto lg:max-w-lg lg:px-10 lg:py-12"
    >
      <h1 data-hero-reveal class="text-h3 text-paper sm:text-h2">
        Security You Can Rely On, Every Hour of the Week
      </h1>
      <p data-hero-reveal class="text-caption text-paper mt-5 max-w-md">
        House of Guards supplies SIA licensed officers, stewards and CCTV
        operators to businesses, sites and events across the United Kingdom.
      </p>
      <div data-hero-reveal class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
        <Button href="/contact">Get a Quote</Button>
        <Button href="/services" variant="outline-light">Services We Offer</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
